<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edinburgh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edinburgh is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>. Its castle rock was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/din-eidyn">Din Eidyn</a>, the stronghold of the British kingdom of Gododdin, until the Northumbrians took it, probably in 638. From the 15th century it was Scotland's capital, the seat of the royal court and parliament, and in the 18th century it was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edinburgh.jpg" alt="Edinburgh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Colin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Edinburgh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal burghs such as Berwick, Perth, Aberdeen and <b>Edinburgh</b> held monopolies of foreign trade, exporting wool, hides, fish and later coal and salt to the Low Countries, the Baltic and France; from the fifteenth century a Scottish staple port in the Netherlands, from 1541 at Veere, handled much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished in <b>Edinburgh</b>, Glasgow and Aberdeen, the Italian Enlightenment in Milan and Naples, and in Prussia Kant published his Critique of Pure Reason in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…Francis Brothers and Day of London published it as a work written and composed by James Yorkston of <b>Edinburgh</b>, with music arranged by Edmund Forman; that edition says it was reprinted with permission from Kohler and Son of Edinburgh, which implies an earlier Scottish edition, but no copy…</blockquote><a href="/songs/molly-malone">Molly Malone</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Philip, Duke of <b>Edinburgh</b>, commissioned a setting from William Lovelady for his 75th birthday; an abridged version for four-part choir and organ was sung at his funeral in St George's Chapel, Windsor, on 17 April 2021.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li><li><blockquote>One of the earliest printings of the music was published in <b>Edinburgh</b> in 1840 by Paterson as &quot;Bonnie Loch Loman&quot;, credited to &quot;a lady&quot;, with arrangements by Finlay Dunn and John Thomson.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li><li><blockquote>He was captured and hanged in <b>Edinburgh</b> a month later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
